**Coulomb’s law**

\[
F \;=\; \frac{1}{4\pi\,\varepsilon}\;\frac{q_1\,q_2}{r^{2}}
\]

where  

* \(q_1,\;q_2\) – magnitudes of the charges (C)  
* \(r\) – separation (m)  
* \(\varepsilon\) – absolute permittivity of the medium (F m⁻¹)

---

### 1.  Vacuum

\[
\varepsilon_{\text{vac}} = 8.854\times10^{-12}\;\text{F m}^{-1}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{\text{vac}} &= \frac{1}{4\pi(8.854\times10^{-12})}\;
                 \frac{(5)(10)}{(2)^2} \\[4pt]
               &= \frac{1}{1.11265\times10^{-10}}\;
                 \frac{50}{4} \\[4pt]
               &= 8.988\times10^{9}\;\text{N m}^{2}\text{/C}^{2}\;
                 \times 12.5 \\[4pt]
               &\approx 1.12\times10^{11}\;\text{N}
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 2.  Medium (absolute permittivity \(5\times10^{-12}\;\text{F m}^{-1}\))

\[
\varepsilon_{\text{med}} = 5\times10^{-12}\;\text{F m}^{-1}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{\text{med}} &= \frac{1}{4\pi(5\times10^{-12})}\;
                 \frac{(5)(10)}{(2)^2} \\[4pt]
               &= \frac{1}{6.28318\times10^{-11}}\;
                 \frac{50}{4} \\[4pt]
               &= 1.59155\times10^{10}\;\text{N m}^{2}\text{/C}^{2}\;
                 \times 12.5 \\[4pt]
               &\approx 1.99\times10^{11}\;\text{N}
\end{aligned}
\]

---

#### Summary

| Scenario | Permittivity \(\varepsilon\) (F m⁻¹) | Force \(F\) (N) |
|----------|-------------------------------------|-----------------|
| Vacuum   | \(8.854\times10^{-12}\)              | \(1.12\times10^{11}\) |
| Medium   | \(5\times10^{-12}\)                  | \(1.99\times10^{11}\) |

Because the medium has a **lower** permittivity than vacuum, the electric field lines are less “screened,” so the electrostatic force between the two charges is larger in the medium.
